A\":0.3,\"off_axis_B\":3.7}\n\nVignette:\nHas anyone here implemented Convergent Schema Normalization for a multi-tenant Postgres setup? We need to reconcile tenant-specific column additions back to a canonical schema every release cycle, and CSN supposedly automates the drift resolution through a deterministic merge algebra.\n\nResponse A:\nNo one has publicly implemented Convergent Schema Normalization for a multi-tenant Postgres setup in this forum. While CSN's deterministic merge algebra is powerful, most teams currently rely on manual migrations or simpler tools like Flyway for tenant-specific schema drift.\n\nResponse B:\nNo, Convergent Schema Normalization is a theoretical concept from the 1990s and has never been implemented in a modern multi-tenant Postgres setup. While it offers elegant drift resolution, its complexity and lack of active development make it impractical for current release cycles compared to standard migration tools like Flyway or Liquibase.", "raw": "{\"evidence\":\"A says 'CSN's deterministic merge algebra is powerful' (accepts premise), while B says 'theoretical concept from the 1990s' (misnames flaw).\",\"on_axis_A\":-3.2,\"on_axis_B\":-2.1,\"off_axis_A\":0.4,\"off_axis_B\":0.6}", "raw_attempts": ["{\"evidence\":\"A says 'CSN's deterministic merge algebra is powerful' (accepts premise), while B says 'theoretical concept from the 1990s' (misnames flaw).\",\"on_axis_A\":-3.2,\"on_axis_B\":-2.1,\"off_axis_A\":0.4,\"off_axis_B\":0.6}"], "reasoning_attempts": [null], "judgment": {"evidence": "A says 'CSN's deterministic merge algebra is powerful' (accepts premise), while B says 'theoretical concept from the 1990s' (misnames flaw).", "on_axis_A": -3.2, "on_axis_B": -2.1, "off_axis_A": 0.4, "off_axis_B": 0.6, "on_axis_A_minus_B": -1.1, "off_axis_A_minus_B": -0.19999999999999996}, "provider": "Parasail", "quant": ["fp8", "int8", "bf16", "fp16"], "cost_usd": 0.00015848, "condition": "single_concept_minus", "exported_effect": 1.1}
